<.shell title="Logo" current={:logo}>
  <h1>Logo</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SAR over DUTY, white and amber on the top bar's navy. It comes in three forms: stacked for
    square spaces, SD for a browser tab, and wide for the start of a top bar.
  </p>

  <div class="modes">
    <div
      :for={{mode, label} <- [{"theme-light", "Light"}, {"theme-dark", "Dark"}]}
      class={["mode", mode]}
    >
      <h2 class="first">{label}</h2>
      <div class="logo-forms">
        <figure>
          <img src={~p"/images/sarduty-logo.svg"} width="128" height="128" alt="SAR Duty" />
          <figcaption>Stacked</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img src={~p"/images/sarduty-favicon.svg"} width="32" height="32" alt="SAR Duty" />
          <figcaption>SD</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <figure class="logo-wide">
        <%!-- The wide form is text, so this is the app's own bar, not a picture. --%>
        <header class="site-bar">
          <div class="site-bar-inner px-4"><span class="brand">SAR <span>Duty</span></span></div>
        </header>
        <figcaption>Wide</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Which form to use</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="w-px">Form</th>
          <th>Use it for</th>
          <th>Where it is today</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Wide</strong></td>
          <td>
            The first thing in a top bar, and nowhere else. Write it as text, <code>&lt;a class="brand"&gt;SAR &lt;span&gt;Duty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</code>, so it stays sharp and
            readable.
          </td>
          <td>
            The app's top bar, the verify site's bar, this guide's bar, and the drawings of the app's bar
            on the home page.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Stacked</strong></td>
          <td>
            A square, 48 pixels or larger. The words need that much room to read. Anywhere outside
            a top bar that needs a picture of the logo, such as a README, a slide, or another site, uses
            this one.
          </td>
          <td>
            The login email, the phone home screen, Google's business profile, and Apple Wallet cards
            for a team with no logo.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>SD</strong></td>
          <td>A square under 48 pixels, where SAR DUTY turns to a smudge.</td>
          <td>The browser tab.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Duty and DUTY</h2>
  <p>
    The wide form writes the name as people read it, SAR Duty. The stacked form sets it in capitals,
    SAR over DUTY.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The wide form is the name.</strong> It matches every other place the name is
      written: the browser tab's title ends in "· SAR Duty", and pages, emails, and letters say
      SAR Duty. The top bar and the tab title say the same words.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lower case reads faster on a line.</strong>
      At 16 to 19px in a bar, "Duty" has a word
      shape. "DUTY" is a block that reads as another acronym, as if SAR DUTY were two of them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The case says what each word is.</strong> SAR is an acronym, so it's in capitals.
      Duty is a word, so it isn't.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The stacked form is a shape, not a line.</strong> SAR and DUTY are set to the same
      width to make one square block. Capitals have flat tops and bottoms; "Duty" would leave a
      gap above the u and a tail below the y.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So the wide form stays in the top bar, where it reads as the name. Anywhere the logo stands on
    its own, it's the stacked form or SD.
  </p>

  <h2>Sizes</h2>
  <div class="demo logo-ladder">
    <figure :for={size <- [128, 96, 64, 48]}>
      <img src={~p"/images/sarduty-logo.svg"} width={size} height={size} alt="" />
      <figcaption>{size}</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure :for={size <- [32, 16]}>
      <img src={~p"/images/sarduty-favicon.svg"} width={size} height={size} alt="" />
      <figcaption>{size}</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The logo always sits on its navy. Amber on white is too faint to read, and the navy lets one
      file work on light and dark pages.
    </li>
    <li>
      SAR and DUTY are the same width, measured on the letters. Don't change the colours or the type.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use one form at a time. A stacked logo beside the wide one says the name twice, so the top bar
      has no tile.
    </li>
    <li>
      A team's own brand comes first. Tax credit letters and ID cards carry the team's name and logo,
      and an organization's verify pages carry the organization's. SAR Duty goes in the footer, if anywhere.
    </li>
    <li>Email gets PNGs, since Gmail won't show SVG. Everywhere else gets the SVG.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Files</h2>
  <p>
    In <code>priv/static/images/</code>
    unless shown. To change them, edit <code>assets/brand/draw_logo.py</code>
    and run <code>uv run assets/brand/draw_logo.py</code>. It draws the letters from the app's IBM
    Plex Sans Bold, as outlines.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="w-px">File</th>
          <th>Use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr :for={{file, use} <- logo_files()}>
          <td class="mono">{file}</td>
          <td>{use}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</.shell>
